===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[physically]] [[lay#Verb|lay]] or [[place#Verb|place]] (something) on another [[thing]]; to [[deposit#Verb|deposit]], to [[put#Verb|put]], to [[set#Verb|set]].
##* {{RQ:Homer Chapman Odysseys|year=1857|book=16|page=244|passage=[H]is Svvaine / Strevv'd faire greene Oſiers; and '''impoſ'd''' thereon / A good ſoft Sheepeskin, vvhich made him a Throne.}}
##* {{RQ:Gibbon Roman Empire|volume=II|chapter=Foundation of Constantinople—Political System of [[w:Constantine the Great|Constantine]], and His Successors—Military Discipline—The Palace—The Finances|pages=7–8|pageref=7|passage=It vvas here likevviſe, in a place vvhere the diſtance betvveen the oppoſite banks cannot exceed five hundred paces, that [[w:Xerxes I|Xerxes]] '''impoſed''' a ſtupendous bridge of boats, for the purpoſe of tranſporting into Europe an hundred and ſeventy myriads of barbarians.}}
### {{lb|en|Christianity}} To lay or place (one's [[hand#Noun|hands]]) on someone as a [[blessing#Noun|blessing]], during [[rite#Noun|rites]] of [[confirmation]], [[ordination]], etc.
###* {{RQ:Douay Bible|tome=NT|book=Mark|chapter=10|verse=14 and 16|page=113|passage=&#91;{{w|Jesus}}&#93; ſaid to them, Suffer the litle children to come vnto me, and prohibit them not, for the kingdom of God is for ſuch. {{...}} And embracing them, and '''impoſing''' hands vpon them, he bleſſed them.}}
###* {{RQ:Hooker Laws|year=1611|chapter=Of Confirmation after Baptisme|section=66|page=351|passage=[W]hen [[w:Jacob|Iſraell]] ''bleſſed'' {{w|Ephraim}} and [[w:Manasseh (tribal patriarch)|Manaſſes]] [[w:Joseph (Genesis)|Ioſephs]] ſonnes, hee '''''impoſed''''' vpon them his hands and prayed, {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Taylor Episcopacy|section=3|sectionname=With a Power of Joyning Others and Appointing Successors in the Apostolate|page=13|passage=[T]hat the Apoſtolate might be ſucceſſive and perpetuall, [[w:Jesus|Chriſt]] gave them a ''povver of ordination'', that by '''impoſing''' hands on others they might impart that ''povver'' vvhich they receiued from ''Chriſt''.}}
### {{lb|en|printing}} To lay ([[column#Noun|columns]] or [[page#Noun|pages]] of [[type#Noun|type]], or [[printing#Noun|printing]] [[plate#Noun|plates]]) [[arrange#Verb|arranged]] in a [[proper#Adjective|proper]] [[order#Noun|order]] on the [[bed#Noun|bed]] of a [[press#Noun|press]] or an [[imposing stone]] and [[secure#Verb|secure]] them in a [[chase#Noun|chase]] in [[preparation]] for [[print#Verb|printing]].
###* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Edward H. Knight|Edward H[enry] Knight]]|entry=Imposing|title=Knight’s American Mechanical Dictionary.{{nb...|A Description of Tools, Instruments, Machines, Processes, and Engineering; History of Inventions; General Technical Vocabulary; and Digest of Mechanical Appliances in Science and the Arts. [...] Illustrated with upwards of Seven Thousand Engravings.}}|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Henry Oscar Houghton|Hurd and Houghton]]{{nb...|Cambridge [Mass.]: The Riverside Press.}}|year=1877|volume=!II (GAS–REA)|page=1172|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=HEYOAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA1172|column=1|oclc=1161549507|passage=They [pages] are '''imposed''' as follows, the illustration showing how the pages appear in the form. {{...}} 18, 24, 32, and 48mo may be '''imposed''' in a similar manner, or may be so '''imposed''' as to be cut before folding.}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}}
### To [[apply#Verb|apply]], [[enforce]], or [[establish]] (something, often [[regard#Verb|regarded]] as [[burdensome]] as a [[restriction]] or [[tax#Noun|tax]]: see {{senseno|en|place a burden}}) with [[authority]].
###: {{syn|en|lay}}
###: {{ux|en|Congress '''imposed''' new tariffs.}}
###: {{ux|en|Sanctions were '''imposed''' on the country that had made an unprovoked attack on its neighbour.}}
###* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|year=1596.2|book=V|canto=VIII|stanza=49|page=295|passage=In crueltie and outrage ſhe did pas, / To proue her ſurname true, that ſhe '''impoſed''' has.}}
###* {{RQ:Camden Remaines|chapter=The Inhabitants of Britaine|page=8|passage=Great alſo is the glorie of thoſe Britans, {{...}} For they not only ſeated themſelves, there maugre the Romans, (then indeede lovv, and neare ſetting,) and the French: but alſo '''impoſed''' their name to the countrey, held an defended the ſame againſt the French, vntill in our grandfathers memorie, it vvas vnited to ''France'' by the ſacred bonds of matrimonie.}}
###* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Edmund Waller|Edmond [''i.e.'', Edmund] Waller]]|chapter=To the King on His Navy|title=Poems, &c. Written upon Several Occasions, and to Several Persons.{{nb...|[...] Never till now Corrected and Published with the Approbation of the Author.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} {{w|Henry Herringman}},{{nb...|at the Anchor in the lower-walk of the New Exchange.}}|year=1664|pages=1–2|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1641-1700_poems-c-written-upon-_waller-edmund_1664/page/n8/mode/1up|oclc=228722716|passage=Thou on the deep '''impoſeſt''' Nobler lavvs, / And by that Juſtice haſt remov'd the cauſe / Of thoſe rude tempeſts vvhich for rapine ſent, / Too oft, alas, involv'd the innocent.}}
###* {{RQ:NYT|author=David M. Halbfinger|title=New Jersey reels from storm's thrashing|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230404184216/http://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/01/nyregion/new-jersey-continues-to-cope-with-hurricane-sandy.html|date=31 October 2012|passage=New Jersey was reeling on Wednesday from the impact of Hurricane Sandy, {{...}} Localities across New Jersey '''imposed''' curfews to prevent looting.}}
### {{senseid|en|place a burden}} To place or put (something chiefly [[immaterial#Adjective|immaterial]], especially something regarded as burdensome as a [[duty]], an [[encumbrance]], a [[penalty]], etc.) on another thing or on someone; to [[inflict]], to [[repose#Verb|repose]]; also, to place or put (on someone a chiefly immaterial thing, especially something regarded as burdensome).
###: {{ux|en|Social relations '''impose''' courtesy.}}
###* {{RQ:Guevara North Diall of Princes|edition=revised|book=4|chapter=What Maners and Gestures Beecome the Courtier when Hee Speaketh to the Prince|folio=121|passage=[T]he courtier that proceeds in his matters, rather with oppinion and obſtinacy, then diſcretion and iudgement; ſhall neuer bee in fauor with the Prince, nor yet beeloued in the court. For it is as neceſſary for the courtier, that will ſeeke the fauor of the prince and loue of the court, to '''impoſe''' his tongue to ſylence: as it is to dyſpoſe his body to all maner of ſeruyce.}}
###* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-3|act=IV|scene=iv|page=167|column=1|passage=VVhat Fates '''impoſe''', that men muſt needs abide; / It boots not to reſiſt both vvinde and tide.}}
###* {{RQ:Shakespeare Love's Labour's Lost Q1|act=V|scene=i|sig=K|lines=848–850|page=72|passage=Behold the vvindovv of my hart, mine eye: / VVhat humble ſuite attendes thy anſvvere there, / '''Impoſe''' ſome ſeruice on me for thy Loue.}}
###* {{RQ:Shakespeare Much Ado About Nothing Q|act=I|scene=ii|sig=&#91;H4&#93;|verso=1|page=67|passage=Yet I muſt ſpeake, chooſe your reuenge your ſelfe, / '''Impoſe''' me to vvhat penance your inuention / Can lay vpon my ſinne, yet ſinnd I not, / But in miſtaking.}}
###* {{RQ:Heywood Silver Age|sig=F3|verso=1|page=43|passage=So for your ſake I vvill '''impoſe''' him dangers, / Such and ſo great, that vvithout ''[[w:Jupiter (god)|Ioues]]'' ovvne hand, / He ſhall not haue the povver to ſcatter them.}}
###* {{RQ:Sanderson Sermons|section=14|page=94|passage=[W]here the Spirit of God hath ''manifested'' it ſelfe to any man by the diſtribution of ''gifts'', it is but reaſon, that man ſhould ''manifest'' the ''Spirit'' that is in him, by exerciſing thoſe ''gifts'' in ſome lavvfull Calling. And ſo this ''manifestation of the Spirit'' in my Text, '''impoſeth''' vpon every man the ''Neceſſity of a Calling''.}}
###* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=VII|lines=544–547|sig=Bb|verso=1|page=195|passage=[I]n the day thou eat'ſt {{quote-gloss|the fruit of the {{w|tree of the knowledge of good and evil}}}}, thou di'ſt; / Death is the penaltie '''impos'd''', beware, / And govern well thy appetite, leaſt ſin / Surpriſe thee, and her black attendant Death.}}
###* {{RQ:Bunyan Good News|page=59|passage=I &#91;{{w|Paul the Apostle}}&#93; vvas going to ''Damaſcus'' vvith Letters from the High Prieſt to make Havock of God's People there, as I had made Havock of them in other places. Theſe bloody Letters vvas not '''impoſed''' upon me. I vvent to the High Prieſt and deſired them of him, ''[[w:Acts of the Apostles|Acts]]'' 9. 1, 2. And yet he [God] ſaved me!}}
###* {{RQ:Pope Thebais|page=324|passage=On impious realms, and barb'rous Kings, '''impoſe''' / Thy plagues, and curſe 'em vvith ſuch ſons as thoſe.}}
###* {{RQ:Watts Improvement|chapter=Of Improving the Memory|page=259|passage=Teachers ſhould vviſely judge of the Povver and Conſtitution of Youth, and '''impoſe''' no more on them than they are able to bear vvith Chearfulneſs and Improvement.}}
###* {{RQ:Browning Seraphim|poem=The Island|page=197|passage=We must endure—but not because / The World '''imposeth''' woe. / Prayers hold a better power than dreams / And leave her far and low: / We cannot meet her cruel eyes, / When ours are lifted to the skies— {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|volume=II|chapter=VII|page=212|passage=He forbade his subjects, on pain of his highest displeasure, to molest any religious assembly. He also abrogated all those acts which '''imposed''' any religious test as a qualification for any civil or military office.}}
###* {{RQ:Robert Browning Red Cotton|page=222|passage=Master should measure strength with master, then, / Before the servant be '''imposed''' a task.}}
###* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Harry S. Truman|title=Address at Mechanics Hall in Boston|journal=[[w:Public Papers of the Presidents|Public Papers of the Presidents of the United States]]: Harry S. Truman: Containing the Public Messages, Speeches, and Statements of the President: January 1 to December 31, 1948|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher={{w|Office of the Federal Register}}, {{w|National Archives and Records Administration}}, {{w|General Services Administration}}; [[w:United States Government Publishing Office|United States Government Printing Office]]|year='''1948''' October 27 (date delivered)|year_published=1964|page=884|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=m8EsjgcMsd8C&pg=PA884|column=1|oclc=1023457156|passage=[W]e don't want any Communist government in the United States of America. And if the people of other countries don't want communism, we don't want to see it '''imposed''' upon them against their will.}}
### To [[force#Verb|force]] or put (a thing) on someone or something by [[deceit]] or [[stealth#Noun|stealth]]; to [[foist#Verb|foist]], to [[obtrude]].
###* {{RQ:Evelyn Memoirs|volume=I|entrydate=14 December 1650|pages=248–249|pageref=248|passage=I went to visite Mr. Ratcliffe, in whose lodging was an impostor that had like to have '''impos'd''' upon us a pretended secret of multiplying gold; 'tis certain he had liv'd some time in Paris in extraordinarie splendor, but I found him to be an egregious cheate.}}
### {{lb|en|UK|[[school#Noun|school]]|or|university slang}} To [[subject#Verb|subject]] (a [[student]]) to {{l|en|imposition|t=a [[task#Noun|task]] inflicted as punishment}}.
### {{lb|en|archaic|or|obsolete}} To [[appoint]] (someone) to be in [[authority]] or [[command#Noun|command]] over other [[person#Noun|people]].
### {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[accuse#Verb|accuse]] someone of (a [[crime#Noun|crime]], or a [[sin#Noun|sin]] or other [[wrongdoing#Noun|wrongdoing]]); to [[charge#Verb|charge]], to [[impute]].
###* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 5|act=iv|scene=i|page=84|column=2|passage=So, if a Sonne that is by his Father ſent about Merchandize, doe ſinfully miſcarry vpon the Sea; the imputation of his vvickedneſſe, by your rule, ſhould be '''impoſed''' vpon his Father that ſent him: {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Drayton Poems|sig=Gg3|verso=1|page=461|passage=On him the King (in madneſſe ſo enrag'd) / '''Impoſde''' my death, himſelfe thereto that gag'd.}}
###* {{quote-book|en|author=Thomas of Kempis [''i.e.'', {{w|Thomas à Kempis}}]|chapter=For Passion Sunday. [Of Seven Most Remarkable Points to be Thought upon in Christ’s Passion.]|translator=[anonymous]|title=Viator Christianus, or, The Christian Traveller.{{nb...|[...] Translated from the Original Latin, and Never before Published in These Countries: [...] To which is Prefixed, the Life of the Author.}}|location=Dublin|publisher={{...|Printed for the proprietor, by}} T. Codd,{{nb...|No. 58, Great Britain-street.}}|year=1804|section=paragraph 21|page=148|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=5PZLAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA148|oclc=1063834669|passage=Thou falſely '''impoſeſt''' a capital crime upon him &#91;{{w|Jesus}}&#93;, namely, that he made himſelf a King, whereas he never uſed any royal ornaments, according to the pomp of this world.}}
### {{lb|en|obsolete}} To put (a [[conclusion]] or [[end#Noun|end]]) to something [[definitively]].
###* {{RQ:Homer Chapman Iliads|year=1843|book=XXIIII|page=341|passage=[G]uards vvere held, at all parts, dayes and nights, / For feare of falſe ſurpriſe before, they had '''impoſde''' the crovvne / To theſe ſolemnities.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} ''Chiefly followed by'' '''[[on#Preposition|on]]''' ''or'' '''[[upon]]'''.
## To [[affect#Verb|affect]] [[authoritatively]] or [[forcefully]]; to [[influence#Verb|influence]] [[strongly]].
##* {{RQ:Bacon Essayes|chapter=Of Truth|pages=1–2|pageref=2|passage=But it is not onely the Difficultie, and Labour, vvhich Men take in finding out of ''Truth''; nor againe, that vvhen it is found, it '''impoſeth''' vpon mens Thoughts; that doth bring ''Lies'' in fauour: But a naturall, though corrupt Loue, of the ''Lie'' it ſelfe.}}
##* {{RQ:Pepys Diary|volume=VIII|date=30 March 1669|page=279|passage=But I perceive they do think that I know too much, and shall '''impose''' upon whomever shall come next, and therefore must be removed, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Robert Browning Parleyings|page=253|passage=From no task Thou, Creator, '''imposedst'''! Creation / Revealed me no object, from insect to Man, / But bore Thy hand's impress: {{...}}}}
## To [[encroach#Verb|encroach]] or [[intrude]], especially in a [[manner#Noun|manner]] regarded as [[unfair#Adjective|unfair]] or [[unwarranted]]; to [[presume]], to [[take advantage of]]; also, to be a [[burden#Noun|burden]] or [[inconvenience#Noun|inconvenience]].
##: {{syn|en|presume|}}
##: {{ux|en|I don’t wish to '''impose''' upon you.}}
##* {{RQ:Pepys Diary|volume=VI|date=9 January 1667|page=127|passage=[T]hey do not rob the King of any right he ever had, for he never had a power to do hurt to his people, nor would exercise it: and therefore there is no danger, in the passing this Bill, of '''imposing''' on his prerogative; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|volume=II|chapter=XI|page=129|passage=Your character was unfolded in the recital which I received many months ago from Mr. Wickham. On this subject, what can you have to say? In what imaginary act of friendship can you here defend yourself? or under what misrepresentation can you here '''impose''' upon others?"}}
## To [[practise#Verb|practise]] [[deceit]] or [[stealth#Noun|stealth]]; to [[cheat#Verb|cheat]], to [[deceive]], to [[trick#Verb|trick]].
##* {{RQ:R. F. Burton Arabian Nights|volume=X|chapter=Ma'aruf the Cobbler and His Wife Fatimah|night=994|page=23|passage=Know, that thou '''imposedst''' upon my sire and deceivedst him by dint of thy deluding vaunts, so that of his greed for gain he married me to thee.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} To subject to an [[impost]], [[levy#Noun|levy]], tax, etc.
##* {{RQ:Raleigh Remains|chapter=Prerogative|page=96|passage=To '''impoſe''' upon all things brought into the Kin[g]dome is very ancient: vvhich '''impoſing''' vvhen it hath been continued a certain time, is them called Cuſtomes, becauſe the ſubjects are accuſtomed to pay it, and yet the great taxe upon vvine is ſtill called Impoſt, becauſe it vvas impoſed after the ordinary rate of payment, had laſted many years.}}
##* {{RQ:Robert Browning Prince|page=124|passage=[W]hoso rhymes a sonnet pays a tax, / Who paints a landscape dips brush at his cost, / Who scores a septett true for strings and wind / Mulcted must be—else how should I '''impose''' / Properly, attitudinize aright, / Did such conflicting claims as these divert / Hohenstiel-Schwangau from observing me?}}

